// ⛔ #a-city-terrain-is-what-the-map-paints — WHICH TERRAINS ARE A CITY, ASKED OF THE MAP THAT LOADS.
//
// A terrain was a city only if one of its graphical entries is spawn_city. Millennium Dawn's `supercity` has no graphical entry at all —
// its provinces are painted with the urban index 13 — and so has Hearts of Minecraft's `port`; moving a town into either (urban →
// supercity) ran "uncity" and took the town off the map (MD 12895: 268 of 269 px of city ground → 0, the colour map relit dark), on 3 of
// the 7 installed Workshop maps (review R2). The game draws a city from terrain.bmp, so what a terrain LOOKS like is what the map paints
// under its provinces. Measured 10-21 over every land province of the base game and the seven Workshop maps that own their map: share
// of a terrain's land provinces holding any city ground — the spawn_city terrain 85-99% on every map; Millennium Dawn's and The Digital
// Frontier's supercity 96.4%, Hearts of Minecraft's port 74.5% and fortress 52.9% (none of the three has a graphical entry); every other
// terrain 45.8% or less (Hearts of Minecraft's cherry), every terrain with a graphical entry of its own 20.9% or less (Old World Blues'
// jungle), vanilla's plains 7.5%. So a terrain is a city when its graphical entry is spawn_city, or when more than half of its land
