  //
  // ⛔ #a-port-counts-for-the-province-it-stands-on — AND ONLY THAT ONE PIXEL. The other 56 of those rows were said to "sit one
  // pixel onto the water — which is what a dock looks like", so a row was credited to every land province of its state in the
  // 3x3 around it. That was a Math.round artefact: x and z were ROUNDED here, and read with floor, as every other building
  // reader and the engine read them, all 17,187 naval_base_spawn rows on the six shipping maps stand on their own state's land.
  // The window cost a real finding: it credited a coastal province with a same-state neighbour's port a pixel away. After
  // Generate land + Auto-fix on a vanilla-based mod (batch 8 discovery F9), provinces 7280, 10999 and 11105 kept coastal = true
  // with no port of their own and this rule, and so Auto-fix, said nothing. hoi4.exe (dumpbin; addresses are 1.19.4.1's unless
  // marked) does no such thing: the buildings.txt loader (mapbuildings.cpp 0x141713640) truncates x and z (cvttss2si, at
  // 0x14165FFF8 in 1.19.2) and reads the province at that pixel; one that is not land drops the row ("not over the land -
  // ignoring instance", test at 0x141714062) unless its spawn kind is the one allowed on water (0x1406D0A60: cmp ecx,1); a
  // port row then sets THAT province's port sea zone, [province+0C8h] = column 7 (0x1417148DF); and map.cpp 0x140AB7A70
